  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Providing Liquefied Petroleum Gas (LPG) to Address Cooking Fuel Needs of Rohingya Households in Cox&#x2019;s Bazar</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">2022 marks the fifth year since the Rohingya populations&apos; mass displacement from Myanmar to Bangladesh. The Rohingya continue to face dangers posed by natural hazards, disease outbreaks and a deteriorating protection environment. As the crisis becomes further protracted, the Rohingya living in the camps in Cox&apos;s Bazar are increasingly dependent on aid with little prospect for durable solutions. Significant use of firewood continues, while agricultural land near camps have suffered from siltation and increased risk of flash floods and landslides. Furthermore, growing resentment among the local population on account of the economic, social, and environmental impacts of the influx has begun to threaten social cohesion. In response, IOM established the Safe Access to Fuel and Energy (SAFEPlus) programme to meet the immediate need for safer and sustainable cooking fuel through the provision of Liquefied Petroleum Gas (LPG) to combat deforestation, rehabilitate impacted environments, and reduce health concerns and protection risks. In this project, IOM aims to support 83,247 Rohingya households in 16 camps by providing LPG refills to be used as cooking fuel. LPG assistance is targeting all households, with porter assistance provided to extremely vulnerable individuals who face barriers to carry the cylinders.</narrative>
  </description>
